1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,068
Last time, you made a universe with one named place in it,

2
00:00:04,518 --> 00:00:06,662
and nobody living there but the crew.

3
00:00:07,562 --> 00:00:09,176
Today it gets people.

4
00:00:10,076 --> 00:00:11,376
There are three factions:

5
00:00:11,826 --> 00:00:14,102
one runs the port you start in,

6
00:00:14,552 --> 00:00:16,415
one lives three jumps away,

7
00:00:16,865 --> 00:00:18,653
and one shoots first.

8
00:00:20,453 --> 00:00:23,106
You'll write all three in one chapter,

9
00:00:23,556 --> 00:00:25,394
and then you'll go and meet them.

10
00:00:26,594 --> 00:00:29,839
The template gave me two sides, and I'm deleting both.

11
00:00:30,739 --> 00:00:34,990
But before they go, look at this line: Disposition, hostile.

12
00:00:35,890 --> 00:00:37,691
You'd think that side was an enemy,

13
00:00:38,141 --> 00:00:39,435
and it never was.

14
00:00:40,335 --> 00:00:42,502
The game knows two words on that line,

15
00:00:42,952 --> 00:00:44,970
and hostile isn't one of them.

16
00:00:46,770 --> 00:00:48,799
Lint said clean the whole time.

17
00:00:49,699 --> 00:00:51,110
So keep that in mind today:

18
00:00:51,560 --> 00:00:53,665
clean means the record is well made.

19
00:00:54,115 --> 00:00:57,994
It doesn't mean the words inside it are words the game knows.

20
00:00:59,194 --> 00:01:01,479
A side is a record with three hashes,

21
00:01:01,929 --> 00:01:03,511
a name, and a key.

22
00:01:04,411 --> 00:01:08,170
The key matters, because everything else names this side by it.

23
00:01:09,970 --> 00:01:12,835
Then come its facts, starting with a color.

24
00:01:13,735 --> 00:01:17,929
There's a character, in one word, which decides how they greet the crew.

25
00:01:18,829 --> 00:01:21,343
A disposition, which I'll come back to.

26
00:01:22,243 --> 00:01:25,798
A home, which is a system, written as two numbers.

27
00:01:26,698 --> 00:01:27,831
The jobs they offer,

28
00:01:28,281 --> 00:01:29,736
and the ships they fly.

29
00:01:31,536 --> 00:01:34,229
Now look at the home: zero, zero.

30
00:01:34,679 --> 00:01:36,704
That's where the crew starts.

31
00:01:37,604 --> 00:01:40,689
So the station the game put there last time

32
00:01:41,139 --> 00:01:44,505
now belongs to these people, and carries their name.

33
00:01:45,705 --> 00:01:46,781
Here are the other two.

34
00:01:47,681 --> 00:01:50,386
The second colony is neutral, like the first,

35
00:01:50,836 --> 00:01:53,820
and its home is three systems over and one up.

36
00:01:55,620 --> 00:01:58,534
The third one says foe, in small letters.

37
00:01:59,434 --> 00:02:01,293
That's the only other word there is.

38
00:02:02,193 --> 00:02:06,000
Neutral means they talk, and their station offers work.

39
00:02:06,900 --> 00:02:10,212
Foe means they're at war with the crew from the first minute.

40
00:02:12,012 --> 00:02:14,383
And a foe gets things from the game for free.

41
00:02:15,283 --> 00:02:17,254
There's a fleet guarding its home.

42
00:02:18,154 --> 00:02:21,052
And every system the game fills with enemies,

43
00:02:21,502 --> 00:02:22,772
anywhere in the universe,

44
00:02:23,222 --> 00:02:24,746
is filled with their ships.

45
00:02:25,646 --> 00:02:28,688
So one word gives your villains a navy.

46
00:02:29,888 --> 00:02:32,386
My sides live in three systems,

47
00:02:32,836 --> 00:02:35,522
and the crew can only jump where a job sends them.

48
00:02:36,422 --> 00:02:40,210
So I give them two jobs whose whole point is the trip.

49
00:02:42,010 --> 00:02:45,551
These are quests, like the ones you wrote in Class 1,

50
00:02:46,001 --> 00:02:48,951
and they live in a chapter called Narrative.

51
00:02:49,852 --> 00:02:53,990
The new part is this: Done when, reach, and two numbers.

52
00:02:54,890 --> 00:02:57,275
That means, arrive in that system.

53
00:02:58,175 --> 00:03:00,802
And because the quest has somewhere to go,

54
00:03:01,252 --> 00:03:03,418
Helm gets an Engage button on it.

55
00:03:04,618 --> 00:03:07,045
Last comes the economy, and it's a small one.

56
00:03:07,945 --> 00:03:10,118
Crates drift in most systems,

57
00:03:10,568 --> 00:03:13,098
and the game knows five kinds of cargo.

58
00:03:13,998 --> 00:03:16,642
This chapter says which of the five my world has,

59
00:03:17,092 --> 00:03:18,840
and how common each one is.

60
00:03:20,640 --> 00:03:22,553
The weights are just compared with each other.

61
00:03:23,453 --> 00:03:26,789
So with these numbers, about half the crates are ore,

62
00:03:27,239 --> 00:03:29,116
a third are food,

63
00:03:29,566 --> 00:03:31,304
and contraband is rare.

64
00:03:32,204 --> 00:03:34,030
I left gas out altogether,

65
00:03:34,480 --> 00:03:36,521
so there isn't any, anywhere.

66
00:03:38,321 --> 00:03:40,470
That's the whole of what a writer decides here.

67
00:03:41,370 --> 00:03:43,989
The prices in a market belong to the game.

68
00:03:45,189 --> 00:03:48,406
Lint says clean, and today that's only half the check.

69
00:03:49,306 --> 00:03:51,110
So I read down this list by eye.

70
00:03:52,910 --> 00:03:55,337
Disposition is neutral or foe.

71
00:03:56,237 --> 00:04:00,010
Every home is two numbers, and no two sides share one.

72
00:04:00,910 --> 00:04:04,168
Every job after Offers is a key in the Jobs chapter.

73
00:04:05,068 --> 00:04:07,656
Every ship after Flies is one of the six.

74
00:04:08,556 --> 00:04:10,382
Every good is one of the five.

75
00:04:11,282 --> 00:04:14,088
And the numbers after reach are that side's home.

76
00:04:15,288 --> 00:04:17,916
Science scans Hollin Compact first,

77
00:04:18,366 --> 00:04:21,524
because a stranger shows Comms nothing.

78
00:04:22,424 --> 00:04:24,572
Then on Comms, I select it.

79
00:04:25,472 --> 00:04:27,205
There's the work I gave them:

80
00:04:27,655 --> 00:04:29,669
a patrol, and an escort.

81
00:04:30,569 --> 00:04:32,566
Kestrel Relay doesn't have those,

82
00:04:33,016 --> 00:04:35,377
because it's the crew's own.

83
00:04:37,177 --> 00:04:40,056
I take a cargo run, because I'm going to need the money.

84
00:04:40,956 --> 00:04:44,186
On Helm, there are my two leads in the Quest Log.

85
00:04:45,086 --> 00:04:47,171
I fly the cargo run first,

86
00:04:47,621 --> 00:04:50,298
and then I engage The Second Colony.

87
00:04:51,198 --> 00:04:53,655
And here's the Deepwell's own station,

88
00:04:54,105 --> 00:04:56,215
offering an escort and nothing else,

89
00:04:56,665 --> 00:04:58,022
just as I wrote it.

90
00:04:59,222 --> 00:05:00,161
Now the other lead.

91
00:05:01,061 --> 00:05:02,420
This is the Gleaners' home,

92
00:05:02,870 --> 00:05:04,000
and that's their fleet.

93
00:05:05,800 --> 00:05:07,923
On Comms, I select their station,

94
00:05:08,373 --> 00:05:11,276
and there's a button: Negotiate Ceasefire.

95
00:05:12,176 --> 00:05:13,833
It costs six hundred credits.

96
00:05:14,733 --> 00:05:17,250
A new crew starts with five hundred,

97
00:05:17,700 --> 00:05:20,119
which is why I flew that cargo run first.

98
00:05:21,019 --> 00:05:22,837
I press it, and the war is off.

99
00:05:24,637 --> 00:05:26,508
It's the whole side, everywhere,

100
00:05:26,958 --> 00:05:29,033
and it's written into the save.

101
00:05:29,933 --> 00:05:32,264
But it's a ceasefire, and not a friendship.

102
00:05:32,714 --> 00:05:34,337
They still won't give me work.

103
00:05:35,537 --> 00:05:37,421
Now make the three of them yours:

104
00:05:37,871 --> 00:05:40,470
new names, new keys, new homes.

105
00:05:41,370 --> 00:05:43,227
Choose each one's character,

106
00:05:43,677 --> 00:05:45,600
and read its greeting out loud.

107
00:05:46,500 --> 00:05:49,731
Then decide what's common in your world, and what's rare.

108
00:05:51,531 --> 00:05:54,497
Next time, each side gets something it values,

109
00:05:54,947 --> 00:05:58,652
and what the crew does starts to change what people think of them.
